WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.350
Before I built products,
I studied brains.

2
00:00:03.800 --> 00:00:07.100
My doctoral training was in the
Harvard-MIT program in

3
00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:11.190
Health Sciences and Technology,
on an NIH training grant.

4
00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:15.370
Through that and my postdoc,
including at Beth Israel Deaconess,

5
00:00:15.460 --> 00:00:19.270
I looked at how the systems
linking hearing and movement adapt.

6
00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.230
With expertise, and with disorder.

7
00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:26.110
One study compared white-matter
pathways in singers,

8
00:00:26.260 --> 00:00:29.130
instrumentalists, and people
who don't play at all.

9
00:00:29.280 --> 00:00:31.910
That was published in
Frontiers in Neuroscience.

10
00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.600
In 2012, I won an IDEA² grant,

11
00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:39.430
to develop a new approach to
treating aphasia after a stroke.

12
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:42.830
And before all that, at the
University of South Florida,

13
00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:47.370
I worked on an EEG brain-computer
interface for people with ALS.

14
00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:51.370
The tools were DTI, fMRI, and EEG.

15
00:00:52.120 --> 00:00:55.030
The question underneath
was always the same.

16
00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:56.990
How does a brain change?

17
00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:58.610
And can we help it?

18
00:00:59.080 --> 00:01:01.210
If your work touches any of this,

19
00:01:01.300 --> 00:01:05.890
how brains change and how we help
them, I'm always up for a jam session.
